 ## Overview
-CPU-Based Splat Sorting is a method for reordering 3D Gaussian splats by depth on the central processing unit to enable correct front-to-back alpha blending for transparency [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of order-independent transparency in volume rendering by decoupling the sorting phase from the GPU rendering pipeline, allowing the GPU to focus exclusively on rasterization and blending [S1, unofficial]. It differs from GPU-based sorting methods (like bitonic sort) by avoiding the overhead of complex GPU sorting algorithms and instead relying on CPU-side sorting algorithms to produce a sorted order buffer [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+CPU-Based Splat Sorting is a method for ordering volumetric primitives (specifically 3D Gaussian splats) by depth to enable correct front-to-back alpha blending. It solves the problem of rendering transparency in splat-based volume visualization by ensuring that occluded splats do not incorrectly contribute to the final pixel color. It differs from GPU-based sorting methods (like [Cascaded 2-way Merge](7689-cascaded-2-way-merge-for-global-splat-sorting.md)) by offloading the $O(n \log n)$ sorting computation to the CPU, allowing the GPU to dedicate its resources exclusively to rasterization and blending [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. This approach is particularly useful in web environments or when GPU compute shaders are unavailable, though it introduces latency that can cause visual artifacts during rapid camera movement [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method computes a depth metric for each splat, sorts the splats based on this metric, and outputs an index buffer for the GPU to consume.
